# Computed Property Best Practices

## When to Use Computed vs Methods

### Use Computed When:
- Result depends on reactive data and should auto-update
- Result is derived/transformed data
- Result needs caching (expensive calculations)
- Used in template multiple times

### Use Methods When:
- Needs parameters from template
- Performs side effects (API calls, mutations)
- Needs to run every time (no caching wanted)

```typescript
// ✅ COMPUTED - Derived data, cached
const fullName = computed(() => `${user.value.firstName} ${user.value.lastName}`)
const totalPrice = computed(() => items.value.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0))

// ✅ METHOD - Needs parameter
const getItemById = (id: number) => items.value.find(i => i.id === id)

// ✅ METHOD - Side effect
const refreshData = async () => {
  await api.fetchData()
}
```

## Typed Computed Properties

```typescript
import { computed, type ComputedRef } from 'vue'

// Type is inferred
const count = computed(() => items.value.length)  // ComputedRef<number>

// Explicit typing (when inference isn't clear)
const filteredItems = computed<Item[]>(() => {
  return items.value.filter(i => i.isActive)
})

// With nullable source data
const processedData = computed<ProcessedItem[] | null>(() => {
  if (!rawData.value) return null
  return rawData.value.map(transform)
})
```

## Handling Null/Undefined Data

### Guard Against Null Sources

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Null check returns appropriate default
const itemCount = computed(() => {
  if (!items.value) return 0
  return items.value.length
})

const firstItem = computed(() => {
  if (!items.value || items.value.length === 0) return null
  return items.value[0]
})

// ✅ CORRECT - Using optional chaining with nullish coalescing
const totalQuantity = computed(() => {
  return data.value?.items?.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.qty, 0) ?? 0
})

// ❌ WRONG - Will throw if items is null
const itemCount = computed(() => items.value.length)
```

### Array Operations with Null Safety

```typescript
const sortedItems = computed(() => {
  // Return empty array if no data
  if (!items.value || items.value.length === 0) {
    return []
  }
  
  // Safe to operate now
  return [...items.value].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))
})

const groupedByCategory = computed(() => {
  if (!items.value) return {}
  
  return items.value.reduce<Record<string, Item[]>>((groups, item) => {
    const key = item.category || 'uncategorized'
    groups[key] = groups[key] || []
    groups[key].push(item)
    return groups
  }, {})
})
```

## Computed with Multiple Dependencies

```typescript
// Computed automatically tracks all reactive dependencies
const filteredAndSortedItems = computed(() => {
  let result = items.value || []
  
  // Filter by search term
  if (searchTerm.value) {
    const term = searchTerm.value.toLowerCase()
    result = result.filter(i => 
      i.name.toLowerCase().includes(term)
    )
  }
  
  // Filter by category
  if (selectedCategory.value) {
    result = result.filter(i => 
      i.category === selectedCategory.value
    )
  }
  
  // Sort
  if (sortField.value) {
    result = [...result].sort((a, b) => {
      const aVal = a[sortField.value]
      const bVal = b[sortField.value]
      return sortDirection.value === 'asc' 
        ? aVal.localeCompare(bVal)
        : bVal.localeCompare(aVal)
    })
  }
  
  return result
})
```

## Computed Getters with Store Data

```typescript
// In component - computed wraps store getters
const userStore = useUserStore()

// ✅ CORRECT - Computed for reactive store access
const currentUser = computed(() => userStore.user)
const isAdmin = computed(() => userStore.user?.role === 'admin')

// ✅ CORRECT - Call store getter with parameter
const getUserName = (id: number) => {
  const user = userStore.getUserById(id)
  return user?.name ?? 'Unknown'
}

// ❌ WRONG - Direct access loses reactivity in some cases
const user = userStore.user  // May not update reactively
```

## Performance Considerations

### Computed Properties are Cached

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Expensive calculation is cached
const statistics = computed(() => {
  console.log('Computing statistics...')  // Only runs when data changes
  
  return {
    total: data.value.reduce((sum, d) => sum + d.value, 0),
    average: data.value.reduce((sum, d) => sum + d.value, 0) / data.value.length,
    max: Math.max(...data.value.map(d => d.value)),
    min: Math.min(...data.value.map(d => d.value)),
  }
})

// Using in template - only computed ONCE even if accessed multiple times
// <div>Total: {{ statistics.total }}</div>
// <div>Average: {{ statistics.average }}</div>
// <div>Max: {{ statistics.max }}</div>
```

### Avoid Side Effects in Computed

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Side effects in computed
const items = computed(() => {
  console.log('Fetching...')  // Logging is ok for debug
  api.trackView()             // ❌ Side effect!
  localStorage.setItem(...)   // ❌ Side effect!
  return data.value
})

// ✅ CORRECT - Use watch for side effects
watch(data, (newData) => {
  api.trackView()
  localStorage.setItem('data', JSON.stringify(newData))
})
```

### Keep Computed Lightweight

```typescript
// ❌ AVOID - Very expensive in computed
const chartData = computed(() => {
  // 10,000 items × complex transformation
  return hugeDataset.value.map(item => ({
    ...item,
    computed1: expensiveCalculation1(item),
    computed2: expensiveCalculation2(item),
    computed3: expensiveCalculation3(item),
  }))
})

// ✅ BETTER - Use async processing or web workers for very large datasets
const chartData = ref<ProcessedData[]>([])

watch(hugeDataset, async (data) => {
  chartData.value = await processInChunks(data)
}, { immediate: true })
```

## Computed with Props

```typescript
interface Props {
  items: Item[]
  filterType: string
}

const props = defineProps<Props>()

// Computed reacts to prop changes
const filteredItems = computed(() => {
  return props.items.filter(i => i.type === props.filterType)
})

// Convert props to refs for composables
const itemsRef = computed(() => props.items)
const { processed } = useProcessor(itemsRef)
```

## Writable Computed (v-model)

```typescript
// For two-way binding with v-model
const searchQuery = computed({
  get: () => store.searchQuery,
  set: (value: string) => store.setSearchQuery(value)
})

// Usage in template
// <InputText v-model="searchQuery" />
```

## Common Anti-Patterns

### ❌ Computed That Returns New Objects Every Time

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Always returns new array reference
const items = computed(() => [1, 2, 3])  // New array every access

// ✅ CORRECT - Define constant outside or use ref
const items = readonly([1, 2, 3])
// or
const items = ref([1, 2, 3])
```

### ❌ Async Operations in Computed

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Computed can't be async
const userData = computed(async () => {
  return await api.getUser()  // Won't work as expected!
})

// ✅ CORRECT - Use ref + watch/onMounted
const userData = ref<User | null>(null)

onMounted(async () => {
  userData.value = await api.getUser()
})
```

### ❌ Modifying Reactive State in Computed

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Mutating state in computed
const total = computed(() => {
  count.value++  // ❌ Side effect!
  return items.value.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0)
})

// ✅ CORRECT - Pure computation only
const total = computed(() => {
  return items.value.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0)
})
```

## Best Practices Summary

1. **Return early for null/empty data** - Prevents errors and handles edge cases
2. **Use explicit return types** when TypeScript can't infer
3. **Keep computed pure** - No side effects, no mutations
4. **Leverage caching** - Use computed for expensive derived data
5. **Break down complex computed** - Compose smaller computed properties
6. **Don't over-compute** - Simple template expressions are fine inline
